     "result_text_tail": "rters puts at most about $0.6M at risk.\n- **Reaching 105% net revenue retention needs both.** Pro gives a one-time lift of 2.5\u20135.0 points, and fixing mobile churn adds 1.9\u20133.8 points every year. Only the two together reach 105% in the cautious case.\n- **Q2 stays possible.** The deck says to launch in Q2 if engineering can staff Copilot without taking people from mobile. The same applies if the Q4 2026 win rate against Routewise is below 37% with no Routewise discount running.\n\n**Choices I made where you'd normally be asked** (also listed on slide 17):\n- **Cautious case:** I took half the observed buying rate for both options: 7% for the add-on and 16.5% for Pro.\n- **AI usage per Pro account:** 3,600 requests a month, the beta average. That's on the high side, since beta accounts are bigger than average.\n- **Mobile rewrite effect:** I assumed it removes half to all of mobile-related churn; the pack gives no estimate.\n- **Copilot delay:** if mobile goes first, I assumed Copilot slips by the same two quarters.\n- **AI request limit and extra-request price:** 600 requests per seat per month, shared across the account, with extra requests at $20 per 1,000. These are my proposals, to be tested in the trial.\n- **Owners on the decisions slide:** these are suggested roles; the pack doesn't assign anyone.\n\nThe sequencing call is close, and the deck says so on slide 11. Mobile edges it because customers lost to churn are gone for good, while Pro revenue that starts later is only delayed."
